% 37a-codegen-regalloc.weft — local register allocation
%
% Split from 37-codegen.weft (structural refactor, no content changes):
% the conservative BB-local linear-scan allocator (caller/callee-saved).

\section{Register Allocation}
\label{sec:regalloc}

The baseline back end is an \emph{all-spill} model: every virtual register
owns an eight-byte stack slot, and every instruction loads its operands from
those slots and stores its result back.  That is simple and always correct,
but it pays a memory round-trip for every value.  Register allocation removes
the traffic for the values that can safely live in a physical register.

The allocator here is a linear scan over two register banks: the four
caller-saved scratch registers \texttt{x12}--\texttt{x15} and the nine
callee-saved registers \texttt{x19}--\texttt{x27}.  The generated code
otherwise touches only \texttt{x0}--\texttt{x11} and \texttt{x29}, so all
thirteen are free for allocation.  A virtual register is a candidate only when
it is safe beyond any doubt, so the pass allocates a vreg to a physical
register precisely when \emph{both} of these hold:

\begin{itemize}
\item Its defining instruction produces a scalar---integer, pointer, or
      floating.  Integer and pointer values take a general register; floating
      values take a \emph{floating} register from a dedicated bank (a separate
      scan described below), keeping the two kinds out of each other's way.
      Aggregate values are never allocated---they are addressed by pointer.
\item Its live range can be reasoned about with the linear instruction
      numbering.  This is always true for a value confined to a
      \emph{single basic block} (straight-line code, where textual order is
      execution order).  It is \emph{also} true for a value that escapes its
      block, provided the function has no back-edge: with only forward control
      flow the block layout is a topological order, so a definition still
      precedes every use and the interval \texttt{[def,\,last-use]} is an exact
      bound.  A back-edge breaks this---a register freed at a value's textual
      last use could be reused before a loop re-executes an earlier use---so
      when any back-edge is present the pass falls back to the single-block
      rule and leaves cross-block values spilled.  This is the
      \emph{global} step: it lifts the old single-block-only restriction for
      the common loop-free shapes (\texttt{if}/\texttt{else}, \texttt{switch},
      the short-circuit and ternary operators), keeping a value defined before
      a branch in a register through the branch instead of reloading it from
      the frame in each arm.
\end{itemize}

A back-edge is detected structurally: block ids are handed out in creation
order, which is the layout order the back end emits, so a branch is a
back-edge exactly when its target id does not exceed the branching block's id.
A single pass over the blocks sets a per-function \emph{has-loop} flag from
this test.

When the flag is set, cross-block values are still allocated, but by a
different route that needs no liveness at all.  Every vreg has a single
definition, so a value that escapes its block can be given a callee-saved
register \emph{for the whole function}: dedicated end to end, the register is
never reused, which is trivially correct across a back-edge, and being
callee-saved it also survives any call in the loop.  The pass hands out the
nine callee-saved registers to the escaping candidates in order, pins each
one's scan slot busy so the in-order scan leaves it alone, and records it in
the save mask.  The trade is register \emph{pressure}, not correctness: a
value reserved whole-function occupies its register even outside the loop, so
the supply is finite and the linear scan allocates the single-block values
around what remains.  A tighter scheme---reserving only across the live loop,
which needs real liveness---is a later refinement.

Whether the value's live range \emph{crosses a function call} then decides the
bank.  A call clobbers the caller-saved registers but preserves the
callee-saved ones, so a call-free value may take any register (preferring
\texttt{x12}--\texttt{x15}, which cost no frame save), whereas a call-crossing
value is restricted to \texttt{x19}--\texttt{x27}.  Each callee-saved register
the function actually claims is recorded in a mask; the prologue spills exactly
those to a save area placed \emph{above} the vreg slots and alloca storage
(so their offsets, and the frame-relative \texttt{va\_arg}/stack-argument
reads, are unchanged---only \texttt{frame\_size} grows), and each
\texttt{IR\_RET} restores them before tearing the frame down.

The pass makes three walks over the function.  The first computes, per vreg,
its defining block, its last-use position, whether any use escapes the
defining block, and whether a call falls in its live range---the last via a
monotonically increasing call counter, comparing its value at the definition
with its value at each use.  Determining which operand fields are vreg
\emph{uses} mirrors the dead-code eliminator's classification
(\S\ref{sec:dce}); over-counting a use is harmless here (it only lengthens a
live range), so mirroring that liberal rule stays safe.  The second walk is
the loop check above, setting the has-loop flag.  The third walk is the linear
scan itself: at each candidate's definition it frees any register whose
occupant's last use has passed, then claims a free one from the permitted bank
(or leaves the vreg spilled).  The result is a map that the
\texttt{load\_vreg}/\texttt{store\_vreg} family consults---an unallocated vreg
(map entry~0) behaves exactly as in the all-spill model, so the change is
transparent wherever the allocator declines to act.

One instruction writes a candidate vreg without \emph{defining} it: a
struct- or union-returning call.  Its \texttt{DEST} is the pre-existing
sret-buffer alloca (the call writes the aggregate result \emph{through} that
buffer address), so the vreg was already given a register at the alloca.  The
scan must therefore skip a struct-returning call's \texttt{DEST}, exactly as
pass~1 does---a struct result never reaches the def-recording arm.  Were the
scan to treat the call as a def, it would re-run for a vreg already live,
handing it a register freed since the alloca; the alloca's address, meanwhile,
was already emitted into the \emph{original} register, so the two mappings
diverge and the earlier register's current occupant is silently clobbered.
This is precisely the failure \texttt{gcc.c-torture}'s \texttt{20030613-1}
provoked: a struct returned in a loop, copied through a temporary whose buffer
shared a register with a still-live local.  A scalar-returning call, by
contrast, produces a fresh result vreg and is allocated normally.

The mirror concern is keeping that same buffer alive \emph{across} the call.
A \(\le\)16-byte struct result comes back in \texttt{x0}(--\texttt{x1}) and is
unpacked into the buffer \emph{after} the call returns---a use of the buffer's
address vreg that no operand field records (\texttt{DEST} is not an operand).
When the result is assigned, the following copy-out reads the buffer and keeps
it live; but when the result is \emph{discarded} nothing does, so the buffer's
address could take a caller-saved register the call clobbers, and the unpack
would store through a stale (often null) pointer.  Pass~1 therefore records the
\texttt{DEST} of a struct/union-returning call as a use at the just-incremented
call count, marking it call-crossing so it lands in a callee-saved register that
survives the call.  \texttt{gcc.c-torture}'s \texttt{920908-1}---a variadic
function returning a struct, invoked purely for effect---faulted here until then.

The use-marking helper reads the pass's array bases from the stable
callee-saved registers the scan keeps live (\texttt{x20}--\texttt{x24}) and
takes the per-instruction varying values---the used vreg, current block,
position, and call count---as arguments.

\paragraph{Register promotion of scalar locals (mem2reg).}
A C local is lowered as an \texttt{IR\_ALLOCA} with stack storage; every read
is a \texttt{LOAD} and every write a \texttt{STORE} against that slot.  The
allocator already treats the alloca's result vreg as an ordinary candidate---so
today it may hold the \emph{address} of the slot in a register while the loads
and stores still go to memory.  Promotion observes that if the local is a
single-slot integer or pointer whose address never escapes, that same register
can hold the local's \emph{contents} instead, and the memory traffic
disappears.  The escape test is exact in this IR: the alloca's vreg is safe iff
it appears only as a load's address (\texttt{SRC1}) or a store's address
(\texttt{SRC2}); any other appearance is the value produced by \texttt{\&x}.  A
scan after pass~1 marks each eligible alloca (bit \texttt{0x100}) and clears the
mark on the first disqualifying use, and \texttt{volatile} locals are never
marked.  Crucially this reuses the allocation the scan already makes---no new
register is reserved, so promotion adds \emph{zero} register pressure, unlike a
scheme that grabs a register per local up front.  The finalisation step
publishes \texttt{\_kcc\_cg\_promoted[v]}, the physical register the load, store,
and alloca handlers read to route a promoted local's accesses through a register
(and to emit nothing for its now-storage-less \texttt{ALLOCA}).

\paragraph{The floating register bank.}
Floating values get their own linear scan over a dedicated bank so they never
compete with integer values for the general registers and so an FP chain no
longer round-trips through \textsc{fmov} on every access.  The bank is
\texttt{d16}--\texttt{d31} (caller-saved, no prologue cost) for a value whose
range crosses no call, and \texttt{d8}--\texttt{d15} (callee-saved) for a
call-crossing or loop-carried one; \texttt{d0}--\texttt{d7} stay reserved for
argument passing and the FP scratch.  The scan is an exact mirror of the
integer one---same liveness, same single-block/loop-free/loop-carried rules,
same call-decides-the-bank logic---running over its own \texttt{reg\_end}
array and writing \texttt{\_kcc\_cg\_fpregmap} and a floating callee-saved mask.
A floating candidate is tagged in pass~1 (flag bit \texttt{0x8}) so the integer
scan skips it.  The \texttt{load\_vreg}/\texttt{store\_vreg} family consults the
FP map first: a value homed in a \texttt{d} register is moved with \textsc{fmov}
(d-to-d for the FP paths, or bridged to a general register for the integer
paths that materialise a constant or spill a double to memory), and the
prologue saves exactly the \texttt{d8}--\texttt{d15} the scan claimed just above
the general callee-saved area.

@d CodeGen: register allocation @{
// -- kcc_ra_use ---------------------------------------------------------------
// Record one vreg use during pass 1.  x0=vreg, x1=cur_bb, x2=pos, x3=calls.
// Reads bases x20=vreg_n, x21=lastuse, x22=defbb, x23=calldef, x24=flags.
// flags bits: 1=candidate def, 2=escapes its block, 4=crosses a call.
.text
.p2align 2
_kcc_ra_use:
    cmp x0, x20
    b.hs .Lrau_ret               // not a vreg number
    str x2, [x21, x0, lsl #3]     // lastuse[u] = pos
    ldr x4, [x22, x0, lsl #3]     // defbb[u]
    cmp x4, x1
    b.eq .Lrau_cross
    ldr x5, [x24, x0, lsl #3]
    orr x5, x5, #2               // escapes defining block
    str x5, [x24, x0, lsl #3]
.Lrau_cross:
    ldr x4, [x23, x0, lsl #3]     // calldef[u]
    cmp x3, x4
    b.ls .Lrau_ret
    ldr x5, [x24, x0, lsl #3]
    orr x5, x5, #4               // a call lies in [def, use]
    str x5, [x24, x0, lsl #3]
.Lrau_ret:
    ret

// -- kcc_ra_disq --------------------------------------------------------------
// Disqualify a vreg from scalar-local promotion: if x0 is a valid vreg whose
// flags (x1 = flags base, x2 = vreg_n) carry the promotable bit (0x100), clear
// it.  Called for every vreg operand that appears in a position OTHER than
// LOAD.SRC1 / STORE.SRC2 — i.e. any use of a local's address as a value, which
// is exactly the `&x` escape that makes the local non-promotable.
.text
.p2align 2
_kcc_ra_disq:
    cmp x0, x2
    b.hs .Lrd_ret
    ldr x3, [x1, x0, lsl #3]
    tst x3, #0x100
    b.eq .Lrd_ret
    bic x3, x3, #0x100
    str x3, [x1, x0, lsl #3]
.Lrd_ret:
    ret

// -- kcc_cg_regalloc ----------------------------------------------------------
// void kcc_cg_regalloc(ir_func *func)
// Computes the per-function vreg→physreg map and publishes it in
// _kcc_cg_regmap (0 for an unallocated vreg).
//   Stack frame (512 bytes): x29/x30 + x19..x28 saved; locals from #96;
//   integer reg_end[13] at #152, FP reg_end_fp[24] at #256.
.text
.globl _kcc_cg_regalloc
.p2align 2
_kcc_cg_regalloc:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-496]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    stp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    stp x25, x26, [sp, #64]
    stp x27, x28, [sp, #80]

    mov x19, x0                    // func
    ldr x20, [x19, #IRF_VREG_N]   // vreg_n

    // Default to the all-spill map (no allocation) until we build a real one.
    adrp x0, _kcc_cg_regmap@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_cg_regmap@@PAGEOFF
    str xzr, [x0]
    adrp x0, _kcc_cg_promoted@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_cg_promoted@@PAGEOFF
    str xzr, [x0]
    adrp x0, _kcc_cg_callee_mask@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_cg_callee_mask@@PAGEOFF
    str xzr, [x0]
    adrp x0, _kcc_cg_save_count@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_cg_save_count@@PAGEOFF
    str xzr, [x0]
    adrp x0, _kcc_cg_fpregmap@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_cg_fpregmap@@PAGEOFF
    str xzr, [x0]
    adrp x0, _kcc_cg_fp_callee_mask@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_cg_fp_callee_mask@@PAGEOFF
    str xzr, [x0]
    adrp x0, _kcc_cg_fp_save_count@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_cg_fp_save_count@@PAGEOFF
    str xzr, [x0]
    // Default the slot colouring to the identity all-spill layout: no map, and
    // one slot per vreg.  The colouring pass at the end overwrites both once the
    // allocation maps are known.
    adrp x0, _kcc_cg_slotmap@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_cg_slotmap@@PAGEOFF
    str xzr, [x0]
    adrp x0, _kcc_cg_max_slot@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_cg_max_slot@@PAGEOFF
    str x20, [x0]                   // max_slot = vreg_n (identity default)
    cbz x20, .Lra_done             // no vregs

    // Allocate one zeroed block of seven vreg_n-entry arrays (8 bytes each):
    // lastuse, defbb, calldef, flags, map, promoted, fpmap.  promoted is the
    // alloca-vreg → contents-physreg map for mem2reg; fpmap is the floating
    // vreg → d-register map.
    lsl x9, x20, #3               // vreg_n * 8  (one array's byte size)
    mov x11, #7
    mul x0, x9, x11               // vreg_n*8 * 7 = total bytes
    bl _kcc_arena_alloc
    mov x21, x0                    // lastuse base (also block base)
    lsl x9, x20, #3
    add x22, x21, x9               // defbb    = block + vreg_n*8
    add x23, x22, x9               // calldef  = block + vreg_n*16
    add x24, x23, x9               // flags    = block + vreg_n*24
    add x25, x24, x9               // map      = block + vreg_n*32
    //                                promoted = block + vreg_n*40 (= map + n*8)
    //                                fpmap    = block + vreg_n*48 (= map + n*16)

    // Zero the whole block (7*vreg_n entries).
    mov x11, #7
    mul x9, x20, x11             // 7 * vreg_n entries
    mov x10, x21
.Lra_zero:
    cbz x9, .Lra_zeroed
    str xzr, [x10], #8
    sub x9, x9, #1
    b .Lra_zero
.Lra_zeroed:

    // ---- Pass 1: liveness / candidate analysis ----
    ldr x0, [x19, #IRF_BLOCKS]
    ldr x1, [x0, #VEC_COUNT]
    str x1, [sp, #112]            // bb_count
    str xzr, [sp, #104]          // bb_index
    mov x27, #0                    // pos
    mov x28, #0                    // calls_so_far
.Lp1_bb:
    ldr x0, [sp, #104]
    ldr x1, [sp, #112]
    cmp x0, x1
    b.hs .Lp1_done
    str x0, [sp, #96]            // cur_bb = bb_index
    ldr x0, [x19, #IRF_BLOCKS]
    ldr x1, [sp, #104]
    bl _kcc_vec_get
    ldr x26, [x0]                 // BB ptr
    ldr x26, [x26, #BB_FIRST]    // first instruction
.Lp1_instr:
    cbz x26, .Lp1_next_bb

    // --- SRC1 use? (skip CONST, CALL, BR, GLOBAL_REF) ---
    ldr x9, [x26, #IR_OP]
    cmp x9, #IR_ASM
    b.eq .Lp1_asmops
    cmp x9, #IR_CONST
    b.eq .Lp1_src2
    cmp x9, #IR_CALL
    b.eq .Lp1_src2
    cmp x9, #IR_BR
    b.eq .Lp1_src2
    cmp x9, #IR_GLOBAL_REF
    b.eq .Lp1_src2
    ldr x0, [x26, #IR_SRC1]
    ldr x1, [sp, #96]
    mov x2, x27
    mov x3, x28
    bl _kcc_ra_use
.Lp1_src2:
    ldr x9, [x26, #IR_OP]
    cmp x9, #IR_CALL
    b.eq .Lp1_callargs
    cmp x9, #IR_CALL_IND
    b.eq .Lp1_callargs
    cmp x9, #IR_BR_COND
    b.eq .Lp1_bccond
    cmp x9, #IR_BR
    b.eq .Lp1_defck
    cmp x9, #IR_CONST
    b.eq .Lp1_defck
    ldr x0, [x26, #IR_SRC2]
    ldr x1, [sp, #96]
    mov x2, x27
    mov x3, x28
    bl _kcc_ra_use
    b .Lp1_defck

.Lp1_bccond:
    ldr x0, [x26, #IR_DEST]      // condition vreg is a use
    ldr x1, [sp, #96]
    mov x2, x27
    mov x3, x28
    bl _kcc_ra_use
    b .Lp1_next                   // BR_COND defines nothing

.Lp1_asmops:
    // Inline asm operands are carried on the AST node (IR_TYPE), not in the
    // SRC fields.  Record each operand's value and address vreg as a use so an
    // input value stays live up to the asm, and an output lvalue's address is
    // seen to escape (which keeps the local in memory, since the asm stores its
    // result through that address rather than promoting the local to a
    // register).  x9-x28 survive the leaf _kcc_ra_use; the cursor rides x10.
    ldr x10, [x26, #IR_TYPE]     // asm node
    cbz x10, .Lp1_next
    ldr x10, [x10, #AST_F2]      // first operand
.Lp1_asmop_loop:
    cbz x10, .Lp1_next
    ldr x0, [x10, #AST_F2]       // value half
    and x0, x0, #0xffffffff
    cbz x0, .Lp1_asmop_addr
    sub x0, x0, #1
    ldr x1, [sp, #96]
    mov x2, x27
    mov x3, x28
    bl _kcc_ra_use
.Lp1_asmop_addr:
    ldr x0, [x10, #AST_F3]       // address half
    and x0, x0, #0xffffffff
    cbz x0, .Lp1_asmop_next
    sub x0, x0, #1
    ldr x1, [sp, #96]
    mov x2, x27
    mov x3, x28
    bl _kcc_ra_use
.Lp1_asmop_next:
    ldr x10, [x10, #AST_F4]
    b .Lp1_asmop_loop

.Lp1_callargs:
    ldr x0, [x26, #IR_SRC2]      // args array
    cbz x0, .Lp1_call_inc
    str x0, [sp, #128]           // args ptr
    ldr x1, [x0]
    and x1, x1, #0xffffffff       // arg count
    str x1, [sp, #136]
    str xzr, [sp, #144]          // i = 0
.Lp1_arg:
    ldr x5, [sp, #144]
    ldr x6, [sp, #136]
    cmp x5, x6
    b.hs .Lp1_call_inc
    ldr x7, [sp, #128]
    lsl x8, x5, #1
    add x8, x8, #1               // 1 + i*2
    ldr x0, [x7, x8, lsl #3]     // arg vreg
    ldr x1, [sp, #96]
    mov x2, x27
    mov x3, x28
    bl _kcc_ra_use
    ldr x5, [sp, #144]
    add x5, x5, #1
    str x5, [sp, #144]
    b .Lp1_arg
.Lp1_call_inc:
    add x28, x28, #1             // a call clobbers the caller-saved pool
    // A struct/union-returning call whose result is <=16B is unpacked from
    // x0(-x1) into the DEST buffer AFTER the call returns.  That post-call write
    // is a use of DEST the operand scan never sees (DEST is not an IR operand
    // field), so without help the buffer's address vreg looks dead at the call
    // and may take a caller-saved register the call then clobbers --- the unpack
    // stores through a stale (often 0) pointer and faults.  This bit only when
    // the result is discarded: an assigned result already reads the buffer after
    // the call (`r = f()` copies it out), which keeps it live.  Record DEST as a
    // use at the just-incremented call count so it is marked call-crossing and
    // lands in a callee-saved register that survives the call.
    ldr x9, [x26, #IR_OP]
    cmp x9, #IR_CALL
    b.eq .Lp1_ci_structret
    cmp x9, #IR_CALL_IND
    b.ne .Lp1_defck
.Lp1_ci_structret:
    ldr x9, [x26, #IR_TYPE]
    cbz x9, .Lp1_defck
    ldr x9, [x9]                  // TY_KIND
    cmp x9, #14                   // STRUCT
    b.eq .Lp1_ci_use
    cmp x9, #15                   // UNION
    b.ne .Lp1_defck
.Lp1_ci_use:
    ldr x0, [x26, #IR_DEST]      // sret buffer vreg
    ldr x1, [sp, #96]            // cur_bb
    mov x2, x27                  // pos
    mov x3, x28                  // call count (already incremented → crossing)
    bl _kcc_ra_use
    // Also give the buffer a DEDICATED spill slot.  Marking it call-crossing
    // steers the register allocator, but a buffer that still spills (a nested
    // aggregate-returning call inside a busy expression) is coloured by the slot
    // pass, which recycles a single-block value's slot once its recorded last
    // use passes.  The post-call unpack reads the buffer's address from that
    // slot AFTER the call, a use the operand scan cannot see, so a constant or
    // GEP evaluated for a later argument could seize the slot and the unpack
    // would store through it (the address became a small integer and faulted).
    // The escape bit forces the colourer to dedicate the slot and never reuse it.
    ldr x0, [x26, #IR_DEST]
    cmp x0, x20
    b.hs .Lp1_defck
    ldr x1, [x24, x0, lsl #3]
    orr x1, x1, #2               // escapes → dedicated slot
    str x1, [x24, x0, lsl #3]
    b .Lp1_defck

.Lp1_defck:
    ldr x9, [x26, #IR_OP]
    cmp x9, #IR_BR
    b.eq .Lp1_next
    cmp x9, #IR_STORE
    b.eq .Lp1_next
    cmp x9, #IR_RET
    b.eq .Lp1_next
    cmp x9, #IR_ALLOCA
    b.eq .Lp1_defok              // an alloca's result is an address (integer bank),
                                 // regardless of the element type it points at
    ldr x10, [x26, #IR_TYPE]
    cbz x10, .Lp1_next
    ldr x11, [x10]               // TY_KIND
    cmp x11, #2
    b.lo .Lp1_next
    cmp x11, #7                   // BOOL..LLONG
    b.ls .Lp1_defok
    cmp x11, #10                  // FLOAT..LDOUBLE → allocated to the FP bank
    b.ls .Lp1_defok_fp
    cmp x11, #11                  // PTR
    b.eq .Lp1_defok
    cmp x11, #16                  // ENUM
    b.eq .Lp1_defok
    b .Lp1_next
.Lp1_defok_fp:
    // A floating candidate is tagged with an extra bit (0x8) so the integer
    // linear scan skips it and the dedicated FP scan claims a d-register.
    ldr x0, [x26, #IR_DEST]
    cmp x0, x20
    b.hs .Lp1_next
    ldr x2, [x24, x0, lsl #3]
    orr x2, x2, #0x8             // FP candidate
    str x2, [x24, x0, lsl #3]
    // fall through to record defbb/calldef and the candidate bit
.Lp1_defok:
    ldr x0, [x26, #IR_DEST]
    cmp x0, x20
    b.hs .Lp1_next
    ldr x1, [sp, #96]
    str x1, [x22, x0, lsl #3]     // defbb[dest]
    str x28, [x23, x0, lsl #3]    // calldef[dest]
    ldr x2, [x24, x0, lsl #3]
    orr x2, x2, #1               // candidate def
    str x2, [x24, x0, lsl #3]
.Lp1_next:
    add x27, x27, #1
    ldr x26, [x26, #IR_NEXT]
    b .Lp1_instr
.Lp1_next_bb:
    ldr x0, [sp, #104]
    add x0, x0, #1
    str x0, [sp, #104]
    b .Lp1_bb
.Lp1_done:

    // ---- Promotability analysis (mem2reg) ----
    // A scalar local (an IR_ALLOCA of an integer/pointer type occupying a
    // single slot) can live in a physical register instead of memory, provided
    // its address never escapes.  In this IR the address escapes exactly when
    // the alloca's vreg appears anywhere other than as the address operand of a
    // load (LOAD.SRC1) or a store (STORE.SRC2) --- i.e. as a plain value, which
    // is what `&x` produces.  We mark each eligible alloca with flags bit 0x100
    // at its definition, then clear it for every disqualifying use.  Because the
    // register allocator already treats an alloca's vreg as an ordinary
    // candidate (today it holds the slot ADDRESS), promotion needs no new
    // allocation: after the scan we simply reinterpret the register it was given
    // as holding the local's CONTENTS.  This is why promotion adds no register
    // pressure --- the same vregs are allocated either way.  Volatile locals are
    // excluded so every access still touches memory.
    ldr x0, [x19, #IRF_BLOCKS]
    ldr x1, [x0, #VEC_COUNT]
    str x1, [sp, #112]
    str xzr, [sp, #104]
.Lpr_bb:
    ldr x0, [sp, #104]
    ldr x1, [sp, #112]
    cmp x0, x1
    b.hs .Lpr_done
    ldr x0, [x19, #IRF_BLOCKS]
    ldr x1, [sp, #104]
    bl _kcc_vec_get
    ldr x26, [x0]
    ldr x26, [x26, #BB_FIRST]
.Lpr_instr:
    cbz x26, .Lpr_next_bb
    ldr x9, [x26, #IR_OP]
    cmp x9, #IR_ASM
    b.eq .Lpr_asm
    cmp x9, #IR_ALLOCA
    b.eq .Lpr_alloca
    cmp x9, #IR_STORE
    b.eq .Lpr_store
    cmp x9, #IR_LOAD
    b.eq .Lpr_next_i             // LOAD.SRC1 is an allowed address use
    cmp x9, #IR_BR_COND
    b.eq .Lpr_brcond
    cmp x9, #IR_RET
    b.eq .Lpr_ret
    cmp x9, #IR_CALL
    b.eq .Lpr_call
    cmp x9, #IR_CALL_IND
    b.eq .Lpr_callind
    cmp x9, #IR_VA_START
    b.eq .Lpr_next_i             // SRC2 = fixed count (immediate), no vreg src
    cmp x9, #IR_CONST
    b.eq .Lpr_next_i
    cmp x9, #IR_NOP
    b.eq .Lpr_next_i
    cmp x9, #IR_GLOBAL_REF
    b.eq .Lpr_next_i
    cmp x9, #IR_STRING_LIT
    b.eq .Lpr_next_i
    cmp x9, #IR_BR
    b.eq .Lpr_next_i
    cmp x9, #IR_NEG
    b.eq .Lpr_unary
    cmp x9, #IR_NOT
    b.eq .Lpr_unary
    cmp x9, #50                   // SEXT..FPROUND (50-57): SRC1 value, SRC2 width
    b.lo .Lpr_binary             // everything else <50 (arith/cmp/GEP): SRC1+SRC2
    b .Lpr_unary

.Lpr_alloca:
    ldr x10, [x26, #IR_TYPE]     // element type
    cbz x10, .Lpr_next_i
    ldr x11, [x10, #TY_QUALS]
    tst x11, #2                   // volatile → never promote
    b.ne .Lpr_next_i
    ldr x11, [x10]               // TY_KIND
    cmp x11, #2                   // < BOOL → not a scalar (VOID)
    b.lo .Lpr_next_i
    cmp x11, #7                   // BOOL..LLONG
    b.ls .Lpr_alloca_ok
    cmp x11, #11                  // PTR
    b.eq .Lpr_alloca_ok
    cmp x11, #16                  // ENUM
    b.eq .Lpr_alloca_ok
    b .Lpr_next_i                 // FLOAT/DOUBLE/aggregate → not promoted here
.Lpr_alloca_ok:
    ldr x11, [x26, #IR_SRC1]     // slot count
    cmp x11, #1
    b.hi .Lpr_next_i             // multi-slot → not a single-register scalar
    ldr x0, [x26, #IR_DEST]      // alloca vreg
    cmp x0, x20
    b.hs .Lpr_next_i
    ldr x3, [x24, x0, lsl #3]
    orr x3, x3, #0x100
    str x3, [x24, x0, lsl #3]
    b .Lpr_next_i

.Lpr_store:
    ldr x0, [x26, #IR_SRC1]      // stored VALUE (SRC2 address is allowed)
    mov x1, x24
    mov x2, x20
    bl _kcc_ra_disq
    b .Lpr_next_i
.Lpr_brcond:
    ldr x0, [x26, #IR_DEST]      // condition vreg
    mov x1, x24
    mov x2, x20
    bl _kcc_ra_disq
    b .Lpr_next_i
.Lpr_ret:
    ldr x10, [x26, #IR_TYPE]
    cbz x10, .Lpr_next_i         // void return: SRC1 carries no value vreg
    ldr x11, [x10]
    cmp x11, #1                   // VOID
    b.eq .Lpr_next_i
    ldr x0, [x26, #IR_SRC1]      // returned value vreg
    mov x1, x24
    mov x2, x20
    bl _kcc_ra_disq
    b .Lpr_next_i
.Lpr_callind:
    ldr x0, [x26, #IR_SRC1]      // indirect callee target vreg
    mov x1, x24
    mov x2, x20
    bl _kcc_ra_disq
    // fall through to argument disqualification
.Lpr_call:
    ldr x7, [x26, #IR_SRC2]      // args array (count + vreg pairs)
    cbz x7, .Lpr_next_i
    ldr x8, [x7]
    and x8, x8, #0xffffffff       // arg count
    mov x5, #0
.Lpr_carg:
    cmp x5, x8
    b.hs .Lpr_next_i
    lsl x6, x5, #1
    add x6, x6, #1               // 1 + i*2
    ldr x0, [x7, x6, lsl #3]     // arg vreg
    mov x1, x24
    mov x2, x20
    str x5, [sp, #128]
    str x7, [sp, #136]
    str x8, [sp, #144]
    bl _kcc_ra_disq
    ldr x5, [sp, #128]
    ldr x7, [sp, #136]
    ldr x8, [sp, #144]
    add x5, x5, #1
    b .Lpr_carg
.Lpr_binary:
    ldr x0, [x26, #IR_SRC1]
    mov x1, x24
    mov x2, x20
    bl _kcc_ra_disq
    ldr x0, [x26, #IR_SRC2]
    mov x1, x24
    mov x2, x20
    bl _kcc_ra_disq
    b .Lpr_next_i
.Lpr_asm:
    // An extended-asm output takes the address of its lvalue (&x), which is a
    // disqualifying use: the local must stay in memory so the asm can store its
    // result through that address.  Disqualify each operand's address vreg.
    ldr x10, [x26, #IR_TYPE]     // asm node
    cbz x10, .Lpr_next_i
    ldr x10, [x10, #AST_F2]      // first operand
.Lpr_asm_loop:
    cbz x10, .Lpr_next_i
    ldr x0, [x10, #AST_F3]       // address half
    and x0, x0, #0xffffffff
    cbz x0, .Lpr_asm_next
    sub x0, x0, #1
    mov x1, x24
    mov x2, x20
    bl _kcc_ra_disq
.Lpr_asm_next:
    ldr x10, [x10, #AST_F4]
    b .Lpr_asm_loop

.Lpr_unary:
    ldr x0, [x26, #IR_SRC1]
    mov x1, x24
    mov x2, x20
    bl _kcc_ra_disq
.Lpr_next_i:
    ldr x26, [x26, #IR_NEXT]
    b .Lpr_instr
.Lpr_next_bb:
    ldr x0, [sp, #104]
    add x0, x0, #1
    str x0, [sp, #104]
    b .Lpr_bb
.Lpr_done:

    // ---- Loop detection ----
    // A value whose live range escapes its defining block can be kept in a
    // register across blocks only when the linear instruction numbering is a
    // valid execution order---that is, when the function has no back-edge.  A
    // back-edge is a branch to an earlier-or-equal block; since block ids are
    // handed out in creation (= layout) order, that is simply a branch whose
    // target id does not exceed the branching block's id.  has_loop at
    // [sp,#120] records whether any exists; when it is set, escaping values
    // stay spilled (the conservative local behaviour) and only single-block
    // values are allocated.
    str xzr, [sp, #120]           // has_loop = 0
    ldr x0, [x19, #IRF_BLOCKS]
    ldr x1, [x0, #VEC_COUNT]
    str x1, [sp, #112]
    str xzr, [sp, #104]
.Lhl_bb:
    ldr x0, [sp, #104]
    ldr x1, [sp, #112]
    cmp x0, x1
    b.hs .Lhl_done
    ldr x0, [x19, #IRF_BLOCKS]
    ldr x1, [sp, #104]
    bl _kcc_vec_get
    ldr x2, [x0]                  // BB ptr
    ldr x3, [x2, #BB_ID]          // this block's id
    ldr x4, [x2, #BB_LAST]        // terminator
    cbz x4, .Lhl_next
    ldr x5, [x4, #IR_OP]
    cmp x5, #IR_BR
    b.eq .Lhl_br
    cmp x5, #IR_BR_COND
    b.eq .Lhl_brcond
    b .Lhl_next
.Lhl_br:
    ldr x6, [x4, #IR_SRC1]        // target id
    cmp x6, x3
    b.ls .Lhl_set                 // target <= current → back-edge
    b .Lhl_next
.Lhl_brcond:
    ldr x6, [x4, #IR_SRC1]        // true target
    cmp x6, x3
    b.ls .Lhl_set
    ldr x6, [x4, #IR_SRC2]        // false target
    cmp x6, x3
    b.ls .Lhl_set
    b .Lhl_next
.Lhl_set:
    mov x6, #1
    str x6, [sp, #120]
.Lhl_next:
    ldr x0, [sp, #104]
    add x0, x0, #1
    str x0, [sp, #104]
    b .Lhl_bb
.Lhl_done:

    // ---- Pass 2: linear scan over x12..x15 (caller-saved) and x19..x27
    //      (callee-saved).  reg_end[i] lives at [sp,#152 + i*8]; -1 = free.
    add x1, sp, #152
    mov x9, #-1
    mov x10, #0
.Lp2_rinit:
    cmp x10, #13
    b.ge .Lp2_rinit_done
    str x9, [x1, x10, lsl #3]
    add x10, x10, #1
    b .Lp2_rinit
.Lp2_rinit_done:

    // ---- Loop-carried reservation ----
    // In a function with a back-edge the in-order scan below still declines
    // cross-block values (they need liveness to bound the reservation across
    // the loop).  But every vreg has a single definition, so a cross-block
    // value can be given a callee-saved register for the \emph{whole} function:
    // dedicated for the entire body, it is never reused, so it is correct
    // across the back-edge without any liveness, and being callee-saved it also
    // survives any call in the loop.  We hand out the nine callee-saved
    // registers to the escaping candidates in order, pin each one's scan slot
    // busy so the linear scan leaves it alone, and record it in the save mask.
    ldr x9, [sp, #120]            // has_loop?
    cbz x9, .Lp2_scan_start
    mov x10, #0                    // vreg index
    mov x11, #4                    // next callee-saved slot (index 4 = x19)
.Lp2_wf:
    cmp x10, x20                  // vreg_n
    b.hs .Lp2_scan_start
    cmp x11, #13                  // callee-saved pool exhausted?
    b.hs .Lp2_scan_start
    ldr x12, [x24, x10, lsl #3]   // flags[v]
    tst x12, #0x8                  // FP candidate → handled by the FP scan
    b.ne .Lp2_wf_next
    and x13, x12, #3
    cmp x13, #3                   // candidate AND escapes its block?
    b.ne .Lp2_wf_next
    ldr x13, [x21, x10, lsl #3]   // lastuse[v]
    cbz x13, .Lp2_wf_next         // dead → skip
    add x14, x11, #15             // physreg = 15 + slot (slot 4 → x19)
    str x14, [x25, x10, lsl #3]   // map[v] = physreg
    add x6, sp, #152
    mov x7, #0x40000000           // pin reg_end[slot] busy for the whole scan
    str x7, [x6, x11, lsl #3]
    sub x7, x14, #19              // callee-saved bit index
    mov x8, #1
    lsl x8, x8, x7
    adrp x6, _kcc_cg_callee_mask@@PAGE
    add  x6, x6, _kcc_cg_callee_mask@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x9, [x6]
    orr x9, x9, x8
    str x9, [x6]
    add x11, x11, #1              // consume this callee-saved register
.Lp2_wf_next:
    add x10, x10, #1
    b .Lp2_wf

.Lp2_scan_start:
    ldr x0, [x19, #IRF_BLOCKS]
    ldr x1, [x0, #VEC_COUNT]
    str x1, [sp, #112]
    str xzr, [sp, #104]
    mov x27, #0                    // pos
.Lp2_bb:
    ldr x0, [sp, #104]
    ldr x1, [sp, #112]
    cmp x0, x1
    b.hs .Lp2_done
    ldr x0, [x19, #IRF_BLOCKS]
    ldr x1, [sp, #104]
    bl _kcc_vec_get
    ldr x26, [x0]
    ldr x26, [x26, #BB_FIRST]
.Lp2_instr:
    cbz x26, .Lp2_next_bb
    ldr x9, [x26, #IR_OP]
    cmp x9, #IR_BR
    b.eq .Lp2_next
    cmp x9, #IR_BR_COND
    b.eq .Lp2_next
    cmp x9, #IR_STORE
    b.eq .Lp2_next
    cmp x9, #IR_RET
    b.eq .Lp2_next
    // A struct/union-returning call does not DEFINE its DEST vreg: the vreg is
    // the pre-existing sret-buffer alloca, already assigned a register at the
    // alloca.  The call merely writes THROUGH that buffer address.  Treating the
    // call as a def here would re-run the linear scan for the same vreg at the
    // call site and hand it a register freed since the alloca --- clobbering
    // whatever now lives there while the alloca's address was already emitted
    // into the ORIGINAL register.  Skip it, exactly as pass 1 does (a struct
    // result never reaches .Lp1_defok).  Scalar-returning calls define a fresh
    // vreg and are allocated normally.
    cmp x9, #IR_CALL
    b.eq .Lp2_chkstructret
    cmp x9, #IR_CALL_IND
    b.eq .Lp2_chkstructret
    b .Lp2_defcand
.Lp2_chkstructret:
    ldr x1, [x26, #IR_TYPE]
    cbz x1, .Lp2_defcand
    ldr x1, [x1]                  // TY_KIND
    cmp x1, #14                   // STRUCT
    b.eq .Lp2_next
    cmp x1, #15                   // UNION
    b.eq .Lp2_next
.Lp2_defcand:
    ldr x0, [x26, #IR_DEST]
    cmp x0, x20
    b.hs .Lp2_next
    ldr x1, [x24, x0, lsl #3]     // flags[dest]
    tst x1, #1                    // candidate def?
    b.eq .Lp2_next
    tst x1, #0x8                  // FP candidate → the FP scan handles it
    b.ne .Lp2_next
    tst x1, #2                    // escapes its defining block?
    b.eq .Lp2_gate_ok             // no: a single-block value is always eligible
    ldr x9, [sp, #120]           // yes: allocate across blocks only when loop-free
    cbnz x9, .Lp2_next
.Lp2_gate_ok:
    and x4, x1, #4               // x4 = crossed-a-call bit
    ldr x3, [x21, x0, lsl #3]     // lastuse[dest]
    cmp x3, x27
    b.ls .Lp2_next                // no later use
    // Scan for a free register (reg_end < pos, signed).  A call-crossing
    // value may take only a callee-saved register (index >= 4); a call-free
    // value prefers the caller-saved registers (index 0..3) to spare a frame
    // save, falling through to callee-saved as overflow.
    mov x5, #0
    cbz x4, .Lp2_scan
    mov x5, #4
.Lp2_scan:
    cmp x5, #13
    b.ge .Lp2_next                // no free register → spill
    add x6, sp, #152
    ldr x7, [x6, x5, lsl #3]      // reg_end[i]
    cmp x7, x27
    b.ge .Lp2_scan_next
    // free: physical register = i<4 ? 12+i : 15+i
    cmp x5, #4
    b.lo .Lp2_caller
    add x8, x5, #15
    b .Lp2_assign
.Lp2_caller:
    add x8, x5, #12
.Lp2_assign:
    str x8, [x25, x0, lsl #3]     // map[dest] = physreg
    add x6, sp, #152
    str x3, [x6, x5, lsl #3]      // reg_end[i] = lastuse[dest]
    cmp x5, #4
    b.lo .Lp2_next                // caller-saved: no save needed
    sub x9, x8, #19              // callee-saved: record it in the save mask
    mov x10, #1
    lsl x10, x10, x9
    adrp x6, _kcc_cg_callee_mask@@PAGE
    add  x6, x6, _kcc_cg_callee_mask@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x7, [x6]
    orr x7, x7, x10
    str x7, [x6]
    b .Lp2_next
.Lp2_scan_next:
    add x5, x5, #1
    b .Lp2_scan
.Lp2_next:
    add x27, x27, #1
    ldr x26, [x26, #IR_NEXT]
    b .Lp2_instr
.Lp2_next_bb:
    ldr x0, [sp, #104]
    add x0, x0, #1
    str x0, [sp, #104]
    b .Lp2_bb
.Lp2_done:

    // ---- FP linear scan (dedicated d-register bank) ----
    // Floating candidates (flag bit 0x8) are allocated to a floating register
    // instead of riding in a general register: this frees the general bank for
    // integer values and drops the fmov round-trip on FP-only chains.  The bank
    // is d16..d31 (caller-saved, no prologue cost) for call-free values and
    // d8..d15 (callee-saved, spilled by the prologue) for call-crossing and
    // loop-carried ones.  The scan mirrors the integer one exactly, over its own
    // reg_end_fp[24] at [sp,#256] (slots 0..15 → d16..d31, 16..23 → d8..d15).
    add x1, sp, #256
    mov x9, #-1
    mov x10, #0
.Lpf_rinit:
    cmp x10, #24
    b.ge .Lpf_rinit_done
    str x9, [x1, x10, lsl #3]
    add x10, x10, #1
    b .Lpf_rinit
.Lpf_rinit_done:

    // Loop-carried FP reservation: an escaping floating value in a loopy
    // function gets a callee-saved d-register (d8..d15) for the whole function,
    // correct across the back-edge because every vreg is single-def.
    ldr x9, [sp, #120]            // has_loop?
    cbz x9, .Lpf_scan_start
    mov x10, #0
    mov x11, #16                   // next callee FP slot (16 → d8)
.Lpf_wf:
    cmp x10, x20
    b.hs .Lpf_scan_start
    cmp x11, #24                   // d8..d15 pool exhausted?
    b.hs .Lpf_scan_start
    ldr x12, [x24, x10, lsl #3]   // flags[v]
    tst x12, #0x8                  // FP candidate?
    b.eq .Lpf_wf_next
    and x13, x12, #3
    cmp x13, #3                   // candidate AND escapes its block?
    b.ne .Lpf_wf_next
    ldr x13, [x21, x10, lsl #3]   // lastuse[v]
    cbz x13, .Lpf_wf_next
    sub x14, x11, #8              // d-reg = slot-8 (slot 16 → d8)
    add x6, x25, x20, lsl #4      // fpmap base = map + vreg_n*16
    str x14, [x6, x10, lsl #3]     // fpmap[v] = d-reg
    add x6, sp, #256
    mov x7, #0x40000000           // pin reg_end_fp[slot] for the whole scan
    str x7, [x6, x11, lsl #3]
    sub x7, x14, #8              // fp_callee_mask bit index (d8 → 0)
    mov x8, #1
    lsl x8, x8, x7
    adrp x6, _kcc_cg_fp_callee_mask@@PAGE
    add  x6, x6, _kcc_cg_fp_callee_mask@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x9, [x6]
    orr x9, x9, x8
    str x9, [x6]
    add x11, x11, #1
.Lpf_wf_next:
    add x10, x10, #1
    b .Lpf_wf

.Lpf_scan_start:
    ldr x0, [x19, #IRF_BLOCKS]
    ldr x1, [x0, #VEC_COUNT]
    str x1, [sp, #112]
    str xzr, [sp, #104]
    mov x27, #0                    // pos
.Lpf_bb:
    ldr x0, [sp, #104]
    ldr x1, [sp, #112]
    cmp x0, x1
    b.hs .Lpf_done
    ldr x0, [x19, #IRF_BLOCKS]
    ldr x1, [sp, #104]
    bl _kcc_vec_get
    ldr x26, [x0]
    ldr x26, [x26, #BB_FIRST]
.Lpf_instr:
    cbz x26, .Lpf_next_bb
    ldr x9, [x26, #IR_OP]
    cmp x9, #IR_BR
    b.eq .Lpf_next
    cmp x9, #IR_BR_COND
    b.eq .Lpf_next
    cmp x9, #IR_STORE
    b.eq .Lpf_next
    cmp x9, #IR_RET
    b.eq .Lpf_next
    ldr x0, [x26, #IR_DEST]
    cmp x0, x20
    b.hs .Lpf_next
    ldr x1, [x24, x0, lsl #3]     // flags[dest]
    tst x1, #0x8                  // FP candidate?
    b.eq .Lpf_next
    tst x1, #2                    // escapes its defining block?
    b.eq .Lpf_gate_ok
    ldr x9, [sp, #120]           // escaping → allocate only when loop-free
    cbnz x9, .Lpf_next
.Lpf_gate_ok:
    and x4, x1, #4               // crossed-a-call bit
    ldr x3, [x21, x0, lsl #3]     // lastuse[dest]
    cmp x3, x27
    b.ls .Lpf_next                // no later use
    // Call-crossing → callee d8..d15 only (slot >=16); call-free prefers the
    // caller-saved d16..d31 (slots 0..15) to spare a prologue save.
    mov x5, #0
    cbz x4, .Lpf_findreg
    mov x5, #16
.Lpf_findreg:
    cmp x5, #24
    b.ge .Lpf_next                // no free d-register → spill
    add x6, sp, #256
    ldr x7, [x6, x5, lsl #3]      // reg_end_fp[slot]
    cmp x7, x27
    b.ge .Lpf_findreg_next
    cmp x5, #16
    b.lo .Lpf_caller
    sub x8, x5, #8               // callee: slot 16 → d8
    b .Lpf_assign
.Lpf_caller:
    add x8, x5, #16              // caller: slot 0 → d16
.Lpf_assign:
    add x6, x25, x20, lsl #4      // fpmap base
    str x8, [x6, x0, lsl #3]     // fpmap[dest] = d-reg
    add x6, sp, #256
    str x3, [x6, x5, lsl #3]      // reg_end_fp[slot] = lastuse[dest]
    cmp x5, #16
    b.lo .Lpf_next                // caller-saved d: no prologue save
    sub x9, x8, #8              // callee-saved d8..d15: record in the FP mask
    mov x10, #1
    lsl x10, x10, x9
    adrp x6, _kcc_cg_fp_callee_mask@@PAGE
    add  x6, x6, _kcc_cg_fp_callee_mask@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x7, [x6]
    orr x7, x7, x10
    str x7, [x6]
    b .Lpf_next
.Lpf_findreg_next:
    add x5, x5, #1
    b .Lpf_findreg
.Lpf_next:
    add x27, x27, #1
    ldr x26, [x26, #IR_NEXT]
    b .Lpf_instr
.Lpf_next_bb:
    ldr x0, [sp, #104]
    add x0, x0, #1
    str x0, [sp, #104]
    b .Lpf_bb
.Lpf_done:
    // Publish the FP map and its callee-saved save count.
    add x6, x25, x20, lsl #4      // fpmap base
    adrp x0, _kcc_cg_fpregmap@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_cg_fpregmap@@PAGEOFF
    str x6, [x0]
    adrp x0, _kcc_cg_fp_callee_mask@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_cg_fp_callee_mask@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x0]
    mov x2, #0
.Lpf_pop:
    cbz x1, .Lpf_pop_done
    and x3, x1, #1
    add x2, x2, x3
    lsr x1, x1, #1
    b .Lpf_pop
.Lpf_pop_done:
    adrp x0, _kcc_cg_fp_save_count@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_cg_fp_save_count@@PAGEOFF
    str x2, [x0]

    // Build the promotion map: promoted[v] = map[v] for every alloca vreg the
    // scan actually placed in a register (map[v] != 0) that survived the
    // escape scan (flags bit 0x100).  The register the allocator handed the
    // alloca---which would otherwise hold the slot address---is reinterpreted
    // by the load/store/alloca codegen as the local's live-in-register value.
    // The block was zeroed, so we only write the promoted entries.
    lsl x13, x20, #3
    add x13, x25, x13             // promoted base = map + vreg_n*8
    mov x9, #0
.Lpr_fill:
    cmp x9, x20
    b.hs .Lpr_fill_done
    ldr x10, [x24, x9, lsl #3]    // flags[v]
    tst x10, #0x100
    b.eq .Lpr_fill_next
    ldr x11, [x25, x9, lsl #3]    // map[v]
    cbz x11, .Lpr_fill_next
    str x11, [x13, x9, lsl #3]    // promoted[v] = physreg
.Lpr_fill_next:
    add x9, x9, #1
    b .Lpr_fill
.Lpr_fill_done:
    adrp x0, _kcc_cg_promoted@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_cg_promoted@@PAGEOFF
    str x13, [x0]

    // Publish the finished map.
    adrp x0, _kcc_cg_regmap@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_cg_regmap@@PAGEOFF
    str x25, [x0]

    // save_count = popcount(callee_mask) — how many regs the prologue spills.
    adrp x0, _kcc_cg_callee_mask@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_cg_callee_mask@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x0]
    mov x2, #0
.Lra_popcnt:
    cbz x1, .Lra_popcnt_done
    and x3, x1, #1
    add x2, x2, x3
    lsr x1, x1, #1
    b .Lra_popcnt
.Lra_popcnt_done:
    adrp x0, _kcc_cg_save_count@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_cg_save_count@@PAGEOFF
    str x2, [x0]

    // Colour the spill slots now that the register/FP/promotion maps are final.
    // x19=func, x20=vreg_n, x21=lastuse, x24=flags are still the pass's bases.
    mov x0, x19
    mov x1, x20
    mov x2, x21
    mov x3, x24
    bl _kcc_cg_color_slots

.Lra_done:
    ldp x27, x28, [sp, #80]
    ldp x25, x26, [sp, #64]
    ldp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #496
    ret
@| _kcc_cg_regalloc _kcc_ra_use _kcc_ra_disq
@}

\section{Spill-Slot Colouring}
\label{sec:slotcolour}

The all-spill frame gives every vreg its own eight-byte slot, so a function's
frame grows with its \emph{total} vreg count.  For a small function this is
invisible, but a very large one---Lua's \texttt{luaV\_execute}, a single
function of some fifteen thousand IR instructions---then demands a
$15000 \times 8 \approx 120$\,KB frame.  Two hundred nested C calls through such
a frame overrun the default eight-megabyte stack, so a program that recurses in
C (Lua catching its own stack-overflow, say) faults the hardware stack before
the interpreter's own recursion guard can fire.  A production compiler must not
let a frame scale with the instruction count when most of those values are
short-lived temporaries.

The remedy is to let non-overlapping vregs \emph{share} a slot, exactly as the
register allocator lets them share a register.  The colouring runs right after
the register/FP/promotion maps are final and reuses the liveness the allocator
already computed: the per-vreg last-use position and the ``escapes its block''
flag.  A subtlety governs \emph{which} vregs get a slot.  It is tempting to
colour only the values that stay in memory and skip anything the allocator put
in a register, but that is unsafe: a value the integer allocator kept in a
general register can still have its stack slot addressed---by the
incoming-argument spill, and by the general-register paths that stage a value
through its slot---so leaving it slot-less lets that address land on another
value's slot and corrupt it.  The colouring therefore assigns a slot to
\emph{every} integer value, excluding only the two kinds that provably never
touch an integer slot: a value homed in a \texttt{d}-register (its accesses go
through the FP map) and a promoted scalar local (its accesses become register
moves).  Reuse then shrinks the frame; the register-resident values simply
occupy a slot across their live range like any other.

A single linear scan over the instructions, in the same order and with the same
position numbering pass~1 used, does the work.  At each position it first
\emph{expires} every active single-block vreg whose last use lies strictly
before the current position, returning its slot to a free list; then, if the
instruction defines a slot-needing vreg, it hands that vreg a slot.  ``Defines a
vreg'' is the load-bearing qualifier: only value-producing opcodes carry a vreg
in \texttt{IR\_DEST}.  A \texttt{STORE} keeps its \emph{access-size code} there,
a \texttt{BR\_COND} its condition (a use, not a def); reading either as a fresh
definition would slot a bogus number.  This was not hypothetical---a 16-bit
store's access code~2 posed as ``vreg~2'' and pulled a slot off the free list
mid-block, so under enough pressure a local's address and the constant stored
into it aliased one slot and \texttt{l = 1} became a store of~1 \emph{through}
the value~1, faulting at address~1.  So the scan skips the non-defining opcodes
(\texttt{STORE}, \texttt{BR}, \texttt{BR\_COND}, \texttt{RET}) exactly as pass~1
does before it looks at \texttt{IR\_DEST}.  A value
confined to one basic block (the escape flag clear) is a \emph{reuse}
candidate: it takes a slot off the free list when one is available, and joins
the active set so its slot is recycled once it dies.  This is safe irrespective
of loops---within a single block textual order is execution order, and across a
back-edge every single-block value from the previous iteration is already dead
when the block re-executes, so no live value ever shares a slot.  A value that
escapes its block is given a \emph{dedicated} slot that is never freed; that is
trivially correct and keeps the pass from needing any cross-block liveness.

Incoming parameters need a subtler rule.  The prologue spills all of them to
their slots at function \emph{entry}, where they are simultaneously live, so two
parameters must never share a slot even if the reuse test would otherwise permit
it---were \texttt{db} to expire (in the single-block sense) before \texttt{mx}
is defined, the free-list would hand \texttt{mx} the slot the prologue is about
to reuse for \texttt{db}, and the later spill would clobber the earlier one
before the body ever reads it.  This is exactly how \mbox{SQLite}'s
\texttt{sqlite3StrAccumInit} lost its \texttt{db} argument to \texttt{mx}.  The
incoming parameters are the first \texttt{IRF\_PARAMS} vregs, so the reuse test
short-circuits for them straight to a dedicated slot.  Parameters that never
reach a defining instruction at all are still caught by a final straggler sweep,
which gives every such vreg its own fresh dedicated slot.
The
high-water mark of the slot counter becomes \texttt{\_kcc\_cg\_max\_slot}, the
size of the frame's vreg-value area, and the vreg$\rightarrow$slot array is
published in \texttt{\_kcc\_cg\_slotmap} for \texttt{slot\_offset} to consult.
Because the map defaults to absent and \texttt{max\_slot} to the raw vreg count,
a function the pass declines to touch keeps its exact all-spill layout.

@d CodeGen: slot colouring @{
// -- kcc_cg_color_slots -------------------------------------------------------
// void kcc_cg_color_slots(ir_func *func, uint64_t vreg_n,
//                         uint64_t *lastuse, uint64_t *flags)
// Builds _kcc_cg_slotmap (vreg → reused stack-slot index) and _kcc_cg_max_slot
// (slot count) by a linear-scan colouring of the SPILLED vregs, reusing a slot
// among single-block vregs whose live ranges do not overlap.
//   Frame (192): x29/x30 + x19..x28; locals from #96.
.text
.globl _kcc_cg_color_slots
.p2align 2
_kcc_cg_color_slots:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-192]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    stp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    stp x25, x26, [sp, #64]
    stp x27, x28, [sp, #80]
    mov x19, x0                    // func
    mov x20, x1                    // vreg_n
    mov x21, x2                    // lastuse base
    mov x22, x3                    // flags base

    // Four vreg_n-entry arrays: slotmap, active_vreg, active_slot, free_slot.
    lsl x9, x20, #3               // vreg_n * 8
    lsl x0, x9, #2               // * 4
    bl _kcc_arena_alloc
    mov x23, x0                    // slotmap
    lsl x9, x20, #3
    add x24, x23, x9              // active_vreg
    add x25, x24, x9              // active_slot
    add x26, x25, x9              // free_slot

    // slotmap[v] = -1 (SENTINEL: unassigned) for every v.
    mov x9, #0
    mov x10, #-1
.Lcs_init:
    cmp x9, x20
    b.hs .Lcs_init_done
    str x10, [x23, x9, lsl #3]
    add x9, x9, #1
    b .Lcs_init
.Lcs_init_done:

    // Cache the allocation maps (published by the caller before this runs).
    adrp x0, _kcc_cg_regmap@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_cg_regmap@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    str x0, [sp, #144]
    adrp x0, _kcc_cg_fpregmap@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_cg_fpregmap@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    str x0, [sp, #152]
    adrp x0, _kcc_cg_promoted@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_cg_promoted@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    str x0, [sp, #160]

    str xzr, [sp, #96]           // next_slot
    str xzr, [sp, #104]          // active_count
    str xzr, [sp, #112]          // free_count
    str xzr, [sp, #120]          // pos

    ldr x0, [x19, #IRF_BLOCKS]
    ldr x0, [x0, #VEC_COUNT]
    str x0, [sp, #136]           // bb_count
    str xzr, [sp, #128]          // bb_index
.Lcs_bb:
    ldr x0, [sp, #128]
    ldr x1, [sp, #136]
    cmp x0, x1
    b.hs .Lcs_final
    ldr x0, [x19, #IRF_BLOCKS]
    ldr x1, [sp, #128]
    bl _kcc_vec_get
    ldr x27, [x0]
    ldr x27, [x27, #BB_FIRST]
.Lcs_instr:
    cbz x27, .Lcs_next_bb

    // --- expire active single-block vregs whose lastuse < pos ---
    ldr x28, [sp, #120]          // pos
    ldr x11, [sp, #104]          // active_count
    mov x9, #0                    // read index
    mov x10, #0                   // write index (compaction)
.Lcs_exp:
    cmp x9, x11
    b.hs .Lcs_exp_done
    ldr x12, [x24, x9, lsl #3]    // v
    ldr x13, [x25, x9, lsl #3]    // slot
    ldr x14, [x21, x12, lsl #3]   // lastuse[v]
    cmp x14, x28
    b.hs .Lcs_exp_keep            // still live (lastuse >= pos)
    ldr x15, [sp, #112]          // free_count
    str x13, [x26, x15, lsl #3]   // free_slot[free_count++] = slot
    add x15, x15, #1
    str x15, [sp, #112]
    b .Lcs_exp_adv
.Lcs_exp_keep:
    str x12, [x24, x10, lsl #3]
    str x13, [x25, x10, lsl #3]
    add x10, x10, #1
.Lcs_exp_adv:
    add x9, x9, #1
    b .Lcs_exp
.Lcs_exp_done:
    str x10, [sp, #104]          // active_count = kept

    // --- assign a slot to a spilled DEST ---
    // Only instructions that DEFINE a vreg have a vreg in IR_DEST.  A STORE
    // keeps its access-size code there, a BR_COND its condition vreg (a use, not
    // a def), and BR/RET carry unrelated fields --- reading any of these as a
    // freshly-defined vreg would assign a slot to a bogus (or already-slotted)
    // number and derail the colouring.  A store's access-size code 2 masquerading
    // as "vreg 2", for one, stole a slot mid-block: the address `&l` and the
    // constant it stored aliased one slot, so `l = 1` wrote 1 through the value 1
    // (a store to address 1) and faulted.  Skip the non-defining opcodes exactly
    // as pass 1 does.
    ldr x9, [x27, #IR_OP]
    cmp x9, #IR_STORE
    b.eq .Lcs_adv
    cmp x9, #IR_BR
    b.eq .Lcs_adv
    cmp x9, #IR_BR_COND
    b.eq .Lcs_adv
    cmp x9, #IR_RET
    b.eq .Lcs_adv
    ldr x0, [x27, #IR_DEST]
    cbz x0, .Lcs_adv             // no dest
    cmp x0, x20
    b.hs .Lcs_adv                // out of range
    mov x28, x0                   // d
    // A value homed in a d-register or a promoted local never touches an
    // integer stack slot, so it needs none.  A value the integer allocator put
    // in a general register is NOT excluded: several codegen paths still address
    // its slot (the incoming-argument spill writes each parameter to its slot
    // whether or not it stayed in a register; a general-register value is also
    // materialised through its slot on some paths), so it must own a valid,
    // non-colliding slot for its whole live range---hence it joins the scan.
    ldr x1, [sp, #152]           // fpmap
    cbz x1, .Lcs_chk_pr
    ldr x2, [x1, x28, lsl #3]
    cbnz x2, .Lcs_adv            // in a d-register
.Lcs_chk_pr:
    ldr x1, [sp, #160]           // promoted
    cbz x1, .Lcs_spilled
    ldr x2, [x1, x28, lsl #3]
    cbnz x2, .Lcs_adv            // promoted local (register)
.Lcs_spilled:
    // Parameters are spilled to their slots by the prologue at function ENTRY,
    // where all of them are simultaneously live, so two parameters must never
    // share a slot -- otherwise the later spill clobbers the earlier one before
    // the body runs (SQLite's sqlite3StrAccumInit lost its `db` argument to the
    // `mx` argument this way).  The incoming parameters are the first IRF_PARAMS
    // vregs; give each a dedicated, never-reused slot.
    ldr x1, [x19, #IRF_PARAMS]
    cmp x28, x1
    b.lo .Lcs_dedicated
    ldr x1, [x22, x28, lsl #3]    // flags[d]
    tst x1, #2                    // escapes its block?
    b.ne .Lcs_dedicated
    // single-block: reuse a freed slot if any, else a fresh one; track active.
    ldr x2, [sp, #112]           // free_count
    cbz x2, .Lcs_fresh_sb
    sub x2, x2, #1
    str x2, [sp, #112]
    ldr x3, [x26, x2, lsl #3]     // slot = free_slot[--free_count]
    b .Lcs_sb_have
.Lcs_fresh_sb:
    ldr x3, [sp, #96]
    add x4, x3, #1
    str x4, [sp, #96]            // next_slot++
.Lcs_sb_have:
    str x3, [x23, x28, lsl #3]    // slotmap[d] = slot
    ldr x2, [sp, #104]
    str x28, [x24, x2, lsl #3]    // active_vreg[active_count] = d
    str x3, [x25, x2, lsl #3]     // active_slot[active_count] = slot
    add x2, x2, #1
    str x2, [sp, #104]
    b .Lcs_adv
.Lcs_dedicated:
    ldr x3, [sp, #96]
    add x4, x3, #1
    str x4, [sp, #96]            // next_slot++ (never freed)
    str x3, [x23, x28, lsl #3]    // slotmap[d] = slot
.Lcs_adv:
    ldr x0, [sp, #120]
    add x0, x0, #1
    str x0, [sp, #120]           // pos++
    ldr x27, [x27, #IR_NEXT]
    b .Lcs_instr
.Lcs_next_bb:
    ldr x0, [sp, #128]
    add x0, x0, #1
    str x0, [sp, #128]
    b .Lcs_bb

.Lcs_final:
    // Straggler sweep: parameters and any other spilled vreg with no defining
    // instruction get a fresh dedicated slot.
    mov x9, #0
.Lcs_str:
    cmp x9, x20
    b.hs .Lcs_publish
    ldr x10, [x23, x9, lsl #3]
    adds xzr, x10, #1            // slotmap[v] == -1 (unassigned)?
    b.ne .Lcs_str_next
    ldr x1, [sp, #152]           // fp-homed → no integer slot
    cbz x1, .Lcs_str_pr
    ldr x2, [x1, x9, lsl #3]
    cbnz x2, .Lcs_str_next
.Lcs_str_pr:
    ldr x1, [sp, #160]
    cbz x1, .Lcs_str_assign
    ldr x2, [x1, x9, lsl #3]
    cbnz x2, .Lcs_str_next
.Lcs_str_assign:
    ldr x3, [sp, #96]
    add x4, x3, #1
    str x4, [sp, #96]
    str x3, [x23, x9, lsl #3]
.Lcs_str_next:
    add x9, x9, #1
    b .Lcs_str

.Lcs_publish:
    adrp x0, _kcc_cg_slotmap@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_cg_slotmap@@PAGEOFF
    str x23, [x0]
    ldr x0, [sp, #96]           // next_slot high-water = slot count
    adrp x1, _kcc_cg_max_slot@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_cg_max_slot@@PAGEOFF
    str x0, [x1]

    ldp x27, x28, [sp, #80]
    ldp x25, x26, [sp, #64]
    ldp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #192
    ret
@| _kcc_cg_color_slots
@}

